  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1)\nMid level ridge extending from the lower Mississippi Valley into the\nGreat Lakes will gradually get suppressed, first by a trough moving\nacross Canada and then as a series of short waves move out of the\nPlains. This will lead to generally zonal flow for the latter part of\nthe week, with perhaps a bit of short wave ridging heading into the\nweekend ahead of an approaching system.\n\nA weak boundary will drop south across the area later today into this\nevening. This will wash out tomorrow night into Wednesday with high\npressure reasserting itself. Another front may lay out across the \nGreat Lakes and sag into the area later in the week. There could be \nan increase in moisture near this second front which would lead to \nmore clouds. Until then, low to mid levels will remain dry, but there\nshould be more extensive cirrus. \n\nTemperatures will remain above normal.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2)\nEnsemble systems are in reasonably good agreement that a short wave \nwill move across the area on Sunday, although there are considerable\ndifferences on what type of surface reflection this may result in.\nNonetheless, deeper moisture is forecast to accompany this resulting\nin the first chance of rain in two weeks. This could develop into the\narea later Saturday night, but the better chance is Sunday into\nSunday night.",
